@andrewsykim 
> On second thought, this could be misleading because kubelet will always attempt to authenticate to ECR repos but `Provide` will fail if there's no session established. Could `Enabled` instead check the availablility of the metadata server and/or the aws credentials on disk `~/.aws/credentials`? Basically a similar check to what already exists but skips the API calls.

Hmm, I'm not sure what assumptions we want to make here.  Metadata isn't guaranteed to be available, and in the cases where its not, we'd need to check everything in the sdk's credential chain (i.e., one possibility is using the 2 env vars: `AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE` and `AWS_ROLE_ARN`).  Probably possible but also sounds brittle.

comments:
@nckturner what do you think about just having `Enabled()` always return true in the ecrProvider?

It looks like the `Provide(image)` function will then only be called, but will immediately short-circuit when it sees that this isn't an ecr image, based on the regex https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/ec36ff40016e1ea612ae364b3b550cd31dd04651/pkg/credentialprovider/aws/aws_credentials.go#L41

That should mean that users can still use ECR from outside of AWS, as long as their AWS credentials are available (e.g. env variables).
